stakeholders = head('Stakeholders and interests', 'Board members, veto participants, ERIS signers, validators, contractors and voting accounts—in one review.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><div class="context-note"><strong>Attribution boundary.</strong> Public handles, roles and addresses are used only where the reviewed records support them. Address-level relationships do not establish exclusive custody, common ownership or intent.</div>
<section id="board"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Phoenix board: four equal seats</h2><p>Each seat has 25 weight; 75% approval means three YES votes. The preserved history contains A1–A289.</p></div>{table(['Director', 'Preserved A1–A289 record', 'Board wallet'], [[r[0], r[1], f'<code>{r[2]}</code>'] for r in director_rows])}<div class="two-col"><article><h3>Beneficiary participation is not limited to one seat</h3><ul><li>A159: 0xphilipp, Vini and Rose approved an action containing their own recurring compensation.</li><li>A249: Vini proposed and joined the minimum YES set for a 16,900-USDC audit payment to Vini’s address; Rose and MB also approved.</li><li>A271: 0xphilipp, MB and Rose enabled Estima’s first 45,000-USDC payment; Vini was absent.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Dissent is also documented</h3><ul><li>Rose supplied the only NO on A272; with Vini absent, it failed.</li><li>Vini and Rose rejected the earlier Estima A258; a reduced A266 later passed.</li><li>0xphilipp and Rose rejected A288. MB’s only preserved NO was on the technically corrected A28.</li></ul></article></div>{source_list([('/record/audit/phoenix_board_votes_all.csv', 'Download the complete four-director voting matrix'), ('/record/audit/phoenix/director_vote_patterns.csv', 'Download pairwise and per-director vote patterns'), ('/record/audit/phoenix/key_proposals.json', 'Download key proposal responses')])}</section>
<section id="think-tank"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Think Tank: six voters, effectively 4-of-6</h2><p>A separate veto body—not merely an advisory group. Revoting is disabled and execution is members-only.</p></div>{table(['Member', 'Wallet', 'P1–P5 participation', 'Relevant disclosed/evidenced relationship'], think_rows)}<p>Across four substantive veto proposals, the five non-technical members supplied only two ballots out of 20 possible ballots, both abstentions. P5 contains no executable message; even four YES votes would not itself execute a Treasury veto.</p><p class="note">The technical member’s abstentions are mitigating facts. Non-participation does not establish motive, and historical membership at every proposal height has not been reconstructed.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/thinktank/votes.csv', 'Download all Think Tank ballots'), ('/record/audit/thinktank/proposals.json', 'Download all five proposals'), ('/record/audit/thinktank/members.json', 'Download current membership')])}</section>
<section id="eris-signers"><div class="section-heading"><h2>ERIS fixed multisig: five voters, 90 total weight</h2><p>The absolute threshold is 70. All recorded ballots in P1–P9 are YES; absences therefore matter when assessing how proposals passed or failed.</p></div>{table(['Attribution used', 'Wallet', 'Weight', 'P1–P9 ballots'], eris_rows)}<p>Proposal 9 passed with exactly 70 weight: the 50-weight signer plus two 10-weight signers. Lion DAO’s account executed it after passage; CW3 execution can be permissionless, so execution alone does not prove membership or coordination.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/eris_multisig_votes_all.csv', 'Download all ERIS multisig ballots'), ('/record/audit/eris_multisig/proposals.json', 'Download all nine proposals'), ('/record/audit/eris_multisig/transactions.json', 'Download preserved multisig transactions')])}</section>
<section id="validators"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Validators, contracts and large holders</h2></div><div class="two-col"><article><h3>Validators are not one completed bloc</h3><p>Galactic Punks, ERIS and Orbital Command voted YES. Smart Stake and Ceres voted VETO. HighStakes voted ABSTAIN. Eleven reviewed allocation-linked validators had no current ballot.</p><p>Only BackBone Labs and cosmosrescue among those unvoted validators had material current hub delegations.</p></article><article><h3>Large balances need disclosure, not attribution by inference</h3><p>The 215.228M-vAMP account remains unattributed. It holds Galactic Punks NFTs and received several direct Galactic Punks transfers, but their reward or payment classification is unresolved.</p><p>NFT holdings, transfers, timing and protocol usage do not establish common ownership.</p></article></div><h3>Contributors and counterparties also belong in the review</h3><p>Recurring core, comms, ambassador and moderator recipients; grant recipients; OTC counterparties; operations contracts; and the ERIS protocol fee account all affect the control environment. Contract and module accounts must not be mislabeled as people.</p><a href="/treasury/">Continue to treasury and counterparty analysis {ARROW}</a></section>{nextlinks(('/voting/', 'Back: 4853 and voting'), ('/treasury/', 'Next: treasury controls ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
page('Stakeholders and interests', stakeholders, '/stakeholders/')

# Treasury and forensic accounting.
treasury = head('Treasury and counterparties', 'A classification-first review of grants, compensation, OTC, validator transfers and deliverables.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><section class="section-grid"><div class="section-intro"><h2>Start with classification</h2><p>Gross flow is not personal income.</p></div><div><p>The reviewed records mix grants, scheduled compensation, reimbursements, protocol operations, locked allocations and asset exchanges. Each needs different evidence. A large LUNA receipt may be an OTC trade; a contract receipt may be liquidity deployment; a scheduled claim may relate to an earlier entitlement period.</p><p>Do not add upstream grants and downstream distributions twice. Do not describe recurring transfers as vote buying without an agreement, communications or other direct evidence.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring validator-to-ERIS transfers</h2><p>Twenty transfers from February through October 2026; ten from each validator account. No memo establishes their purpose.</p></div>{table(['Sender', 'Transfers', 'Total', 'What is missing'], [['Galactic Punks', '10', '116,735 LUNA', 'Agreement, invoice, pricing formula and disclosure history'], ['Lion DAO', '10', '105,227 LUNA', 'Agreement, invoice, pricing formula and disclosure history'], ['<strong>Total</strong>', '<strong>20</strong>', '<strong>221,962 LUNA</strong>', 'Classification remains unresolved']])}<p>The paired timing and rising amounts are review signals, not proof of hosting fees or vote influence. Request the service terms and determine whether they predate 4853.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/payment_summary.json', 'Download the payment summary and all 20 transfer locators'), ('/record/audit/transfers/monthly_validator_to_eris/', 'Open the preserved original transaction directory')])}</section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring payments beyond the board</h2><p>These recipients require the same invoice, entitlement-period and deliverable reconciliation as directors.</p></div>{table(['Recipient', 'Observed pattern', 'Review question'], [['<code>terra1azcp…j2tz2</code>', '65,000 USDC across 13 payments of 5,000', 'Core-work agreement and acceptance'], ['<code>terra12jrv…g7egn</code>', '32,500 USDC across 11 payments, primarily 3,000', 'Comms agreement and outputs'], ['<code>terra1ez76…clsxu</code>', '14,500 USDC across ten payments, primarily 1,500', 'Ambassador scope and outputs'], ['<code>terra1lqqs…meqs</code>', 'Recurring 1,800-USDC moderator schedules', 'Coverage period and approval'], ['<code>terra1xtyq…vy68</code>', 'Recurring 1,500-USDC moderator schedules', 'Coverage period and approval']])}</section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Director compensation and selective October schedules</h2></div><p>The 2026 coverage window records 57,096 USDC to MB, 66,480 USDC to Vini and 46,745.70 USDC to Rose under the report’s classifications. Vini’s total includes a 16,900-USDC audit payment and recurring infra payments. Rose separately received 237,323.047934 LUNA in an OTC trade supplying approximately 0.19657723 WBTC; that is not compensation.</p><p>October actions 118–120 created new Stewardship payments for 0xphilipp, Rose and MB, but not Vini. Separate infra actions continued. The chain record does not explain the omission.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>OTC and related-party execution</h2></div><p>The 50-weight ERIS signer received 795,991.566014 LUNA through Treasury offers 91, 106 and 111. The checked messages are <code>execute_otc</code>, so these are asset exchanges, not salary. The governance questions are price fairness, approval, disclosure and execution quality.</p><p>Other large OTC recipients must likewise be valued using the asset they supplied and execution-time prices. LUNA totals alone do not establish expense, gain or loss.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>A forensic accountant’s next requests</h2></div><ol class="request-list"><li>Complete action exports: recipient, entitlement period, approval, claim, cancellation and transaction.</li><li>Contracts, invoices and pricing for monthly validator transfers and the ERIS invoice to Rebel.</li><li>Statements of work, competing quotes where relevant, measurable deliverables and independent acceptance.</li><li>Related-party OTC terms, supplied assets, market-rate checks and execution timestamps.</li><li>Beneficiary/decider overlap and recusal records for A159, A249, A266 and A271.</li><li>Admin and owner powers over recipients, schedules, fees, code and future voting.</li><li>Account continuity without assuming exclusive key custody; reconcile current and predecessor role wallets.</li><li>Direct wallet-flow graphs that distinguish native transfers, CW20 transfers, contract operations and module accounts.</li></ol></section>{nextlinks(('/stakeholders/', 'Back: stakeholders'), ('/governance/', 'Next: governance controls ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
page('Treasury and counterparties', treasury, '/treasury/')

# Governance findings, verified handover and longer-term reforms.
governance = head('Governance and independent controls', 'What failed or remains incomplete, what worked as a check, and which reforms would reduce dependence on trust.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Approval design and actual dissent</h2></div><p>The four-seat board requires three YES votes. That means any three can approve an action over the fourth seat’s objection or absence. A159, A249 and A271 show why beneficiary disclosure and recusal matter across the whole board.</p><p>A272 proposed removing Vini and reducing the threshold to 60%. It failed: 0xphilipp and MB voted YES, Rose voted NO and Vini was absent. The current captured board remains four seats at 75%. Sequence alone does not establish motive.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>The Think Tank is not providing an effective documented veto</h2></div><p>Four substantive veto proposals had zero YES and zero NO ballots. Only limited abstentions were recorded. The technical member proposed all five and abstained on all five. P5’s empty message means the proposed veto could not execute even if approved.</p><p>This supports a safeguard-design and participation finding. It does not prove deliberate capture. Review historical membership, notifications, proposal construction and whether members understood the rolling-expenditure rules.</p></section>
<section id="handover"><div class="section-heading"><h2>“Update admin” is not the same as “transfer ownership”</h2><p>Fresh state queries distinguish code upgrades from internal configuration authority.</p></div>{table(['Contract / role', 'After ERIS proposal 9', 'Still retained elsewhere'], [['ampLUNA hub CosmWasm admin', 'Phoenix board <code>terra1k8ug…lppjg</code>', '—'], ['ampLUNA hub <code>config.owner</code>', 'Unchanged', '<code>terra1kefa…klgzl</code>; <code>new_owner: null</code>'], ['CW20-ICS20 CosmWasm admin', 'Phoenix board <code>terra1k8ug…lppjg</code>', '—'], ['CW20-ICS20 <code>gov_contract</code>', 'Unchanged', '<code>terra1q0v…tew</code>'], ['Treasury and TLA registry migration admin', 'Not changed by proposal 9', '<code>terra1q0v…tew</code>'], ['TLA registry configuration owner', 'Not changed by proposal 9', '<code>terra1kefa…klgzl</code>; no pending owner']])}<p>Proposal 9 contains exactly two <code>wasm.update_admin</code> messages. Calling it “Transfer Ownership” is therefore incomplete: it transferred code-upgrade control, not all operational/configuration control.</p>{source_list([('https://chainsco.pe/terra2/tx/86E599263604B396BFB0F35F2F4E505E2EA758B024315B1C055C14994E6242D9', 'Proposal 9 execution transaction'), (LCD + '/cosmwasm/wasm/v1/contract/terra10788fkzah89xrdm27zkj5yvhj9x3494lxawzm5qq3vvxcqz2yzaqyd3enk/smart/eyJjb25maWciOnt9fQ==', 'Current hub internal config'), (LCD + '/cosmwasm/wasm/v1/contract/terra1e0mrzy8077druuu42vs0hu7ugguade0cj65dgtauyaw4gsl4kv0qtdf2au/smart/eyJjb25maWciOnt9fQ==', 'Current ICS20 internal config'), ('/record/audit/admin_handover/', 'Download the contract metadata and config captures')])}<h3>Original handover commitments</h3><p>The 2024 TLA launch text says registry ownership transfers after three months and contract admin after one month. The packaged ledger identifies 31 privileged operations in 19 transactions after its conservative cutoff, affecting seven original contracts. The original commitment and current roles were independently checked; every historical ledger operation was not replayed in this review.</p><p class="note">Successful transactions prove use of an address’s authority, not exclusive human key custody. No approved extension was found in the bounded corpus; that is not proof none exists elsewhere.</p></section>
<section id="reforms"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Longer-term reforms</h2><p>Adapted from established governance, disclosure, conflict-of-interest and internal-control principles.</p></div><div class="reform-grid"><article><h3>Disclose and recuse</h3><p>Maintain a current stakeholder register covering grants, compensation, OTC, hosting, delegations and voting power. Require recusal and independent approval for related-party decisions, with the rationale published.</p></article><article><h3>Make oversight operable</h3><p>Give the Think Tank reliable notifications, complete executable messages, adequate voting windows and published rationales. Measure participation and review failed safeguards rather than treating existence as effectiveness.</p></article><article><h3>Separate technical powers</h3><p>Document migration admin, internal owner, treasury control and vote operator separately. Use explicit acceptance for ownership transfers, independent verification and runbooks for emergency action.</p></article><article><h3>Make voting legible</h3><p>Publish hub/gauge intentions and final ballots, validator-mandate rules and material voting-power holders at entity level. Preserve weighted dissent and avoid interpreting delegation as ownership.</p></article><article><h3>Control the full payment lifecycle</h3><p>Link budget, beneficiary, conflicts, deliverable, acceptance, entitlement, claim and valuation. Independently reconcile recurring expenses and related-party OTC execution.</p></article><article><h3>Review and respond</h3><p>Commission periodic independent control review with access to off-chain agreements. Give affected stakeholders a right of response and publish corrections against specific evidence.</p></article></div>{source_list([('https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/g20-oecd-principles-of-corporate-governance-2023_ed750b30-en/full-report/component-8.html', 'G20/OECD Principles: board responsibilities'), ('https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/public/doc/151/body-text.en.html', 'OECD disclosure principles'), ('https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2005/08/managing-conflict-of-interest-in-the-public-sector_g1gh5807.html', 'OECD conflict-of-interest toolkit'), ('https://www.iso.org/standard/65036.html', 'ISO 37000:2021 governance of organizations'), ('https://controlsframework.net/icif/index.php', 'COSO Internal Control—Integrated Framework'), ('https://aisel.aisnet.org/jais/vol19/iss10/1/', 'Beck et al.: blockchain governance research'), ('https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12125', 'Hidden Shortcomings of DAOs'), ('https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/25741292.2023.2270220', 'Research on DAO governance impacts')])}</section>{nextlinks(('/treasury/', 'Back: treasury'), ('/evidence/', 'Next: evidence and method ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
page('Governance and independent controls', governance, '/governance/')

# Evidence, provenance and correction boundaries.
evidence = head('Evidence and method', 'Download the voting histories, inspect original queries, and distinguish confirmed records from inference and open questions.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Voting-history downloads</h2><p>Complete preserved exports within each stated scope.</p></div>{table(['Dataset', 'Coverage', 'Download'], [['Phoenix board', 'A1–A289; all four directors', local('/record/audit/phoenix_board_votes_all.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local('/record/audit/mb_voting_record.json', 'compiled record with per-ballot source locators')], ['Phoenix board, recent', 'A269–A289; last-four-weeks review window', local('/record/audit/phoenix_board_votes_last4weeks.csv', 'CSV')], ['ERIS fixed multisig', 'P1–P9; all five current voters', local('/record/audit/eris_multisig_votes_all.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local('/record/audit/eris_multisig/proposals.json', 'proposal JSON')], ['Think Tank', 'P1–P5; all six current members', local('/record/audit/thinktank/votes.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local('/record/audit/thinktank/proposals.json', 'proposal JSON')], ['Native 4853', '83 current ballots at capture', local('/record/audit/native_4853_votes_all.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local('/record/audit/governance_4853/live_tally.json', 'tally JSON')], ['Lion DAO A33', 'Relationship, voting-power and timing analysis', local('/record/audit/lion_dao/ryan_relationship.json', 'JSON')]])}<p class="note">“All” means all records in the identified proposal range or capture—not every historical transaction on Terra. Current chain state can change after the snapshot.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Evidence labels</h2></div><dl class="definitions"><dt>Confirmed record</dt><dd>A direct chain response, transaction, contract state, source-code line or dated primary document. A successful action proves use of authority, not exclusive key custody.</dd><dd></dd><dt>Derived analysis</dt><dd>Arithmetic, grouping or comparison of cited records. The full-hub-YES outcome and combined vAMP shares are scenarios, not observed events.</dd><dt>Inference / review signal</dt><dd>A relationship or pattern that justifies a question. Timing, payments, shared protocols and NFT collections alone do not establish ownership, coordination or motive.</dd><dt>Unresolved</dt><dd>Missing off-chain agreements, classification, historical membership or notification evidence. No evidence found in a bounded corpus is not proof of universal absence.</dd></dl></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Original records behind the supplied website</h2></div><p>The initial packet came from <a href="https://philipp-payments.pages.dev/">philipp-payments.pages.dev</a>. Its source index and embedded locators were used to identify original chain, forum and GitHub records where possible. Preserved source copies remain under <a href="/record/">/record/</a>; their original commentary is not adopted as this review’s conclusion.</p><ul><li>The compiled A1–A289 record retains per-proposal ballot query URLs, transaction hashes and source hashes.</li><li>The packaged authority ledger retains operation-level transaction locators. The original handover lines and current roles were checked separately.</li><li>Every retained data artifact has a sibling <code>.source.md</code> describing immediate source, upstream source where established, transformation and SHA-256.</li><li>The supplied uncorroborated spreadsheet is not published or used as an attribution source.</li></ul>{source_list([('https://github.com/PFC-Validator/phoenix-forensics', 'Audit repository and provenance register'), ('https://github.com/phoenix-directive/docs', 'Phoenix Directive original governance documents'), ('https://github.com/erisprotocol/contracts-tokenfactory', 'ERIS contracts and voting source'), ('https://forum.phoenix.money/', 'Phoenix governance forum'), ('https://phoenix.money/', 'Phoenix project website')])}</section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Scope and limitations</h2></div><ul><li>Evidence cutoff: 26 October 2026. Proposal 4853 remains open until 31 October at 03:40 UTC.</li><li>Not every packaged historical operation was independently replayed; that limitation is stated wherever the ledger is used.</li><li>Some bounded negative searches are limited by provider pruning and indexing. They are not chain-wide proof of absence.</li><li>Payment coverage and historical valuation vary by category. Gross flows are not net worth, profit or loss.</li><li>No identity research, surname-based searches or personal-contact calls are part of this review.</li></ul></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Corrections and responses</h2></div><p>A credible review must be correctable. Cite the page, exact statement, transaction or proposal, and supporting primary source. Material responses and alternative classifications should be preserved alongside the relevant finding.</p><button class="button secondary" data-share>Copy this page’s link</button><p class="share-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></section>{nextlinks(('/governance/', 'Back: governance reforms'), ('/', 'Return to immediate actions ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
page('Evidence and method', evidence, '/evidence/')

# Compatibility routes for earlier links.
for old, target, label in [('/funding/', '/treasury/', 'Treasury and counterparties'), ('/timeline/', '/governance/', 'Governance chronology and controls'), ('/sources/', '/evidence/', 'Evidence and method')]:
    body = head('This review has been reorganized', 'The earlier person-focused briefing has been replaced by a stakeholder-wide governance review.') + f'<div class="wrap page-content reading"><p><a href="{target}">Continue to {label} {ARROW}</a></p><p>Preserved source records remain available under <a href="/record/">/record/</a>.</p></div>'
    page('Review reorganized', body, old)

privacy = head('Visitor privacy', 'A static evidence website with no sign-in or feedback submission form.') + '''<div class="wrap page-content reading"><p>The review does not add analytics scripts, advertising trackers or cookies. Main pages serve fonts locally. The hosting provider may process normal connection and server-log information.</p><p>Copy-link buttons use your browser clipboard only when pressed. External source links have their own policies.</p></div>'''
page('Visitor privacy', privacy, '/privacy/')

(DIST / '404.html').write_text((DIST / 'index.html').read_text().replace('<main id="main">', '<main id="main"><div class="wrap context-note"><p>This address was not found. Use the review navigation to find the record.</p></div>', 1))
(ROOT / 'source-records' / 'briefing-claims.json').write_text(json.dumps({
    'native_4853': {'source': '/record/audit/native_4853/proposal.json', 'ballots': 83, 'capture': '2026-10-26'},
    'tally': {'source': '/record/audit/governance_4853/live_tally.json', 'yes_non_abstain_percent': 41.62, 'veto_all_percent': 32.57},
    'hub_yes_scenario': {'source': '/record/audit/eris_multisig/hub_delegations.json', 'yes_non_abstain_percent': 55.73, 'status': 'hypothetical'},
    'vamp': {'source': '/record/audit/governance_4853/vamp_review.json', 'large_account_attribution': 'unattributed'},
    'admin_handover': {'source': '/record/audit/admin_handover/', 'proposal': 9, 'wasm_admin_changed': True, 'internal_owner_changed': False},
    'privacy': {'uncorroborated_spreadsheet_published': False, 'identity_research': False},
}, indent=2))

from publication_editorial import apply_editorial
apply_editorial(DIST, page, head)
from publication_privacy import apply_privacy
privacy_report = apply_privacy(DIST)
(ROOT / 'source-records' / 'publication-privacy-check.json').write_text(json.dumps(privacy_report, indent=2))
print('Built process-first review: home, voting, stakeholders, treasury, governance, evidence and privacy.')
print(json.dumps(privacy_report))
